  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Thursday)\nIssued at 225 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThe subtropical ridge will continue to move eastward toward the \nsoutheast United States for next week. The Southern Plains will be\non the western periphery of the ridge with a strong low-level \nthermal ridge remaining in place. As a result, above-normal \ntemperatures are likely with high temperatures in the 90s to mid \n100s deg F. Probabilistic guidance indicates little temperature \nspread through Wednesday, which indicates high confidence of \nabove-normal temperatures (e.g., the 10th percentile is above- \nnormal). \n\nElevated fire weather conditions are possible on Monday afternoon\nwith a strengthening surface pressure gradient resulting in \nbreezy southerly winds. \n\nNorthern and western Oklahoma will be close enough to south to \nsouthwest flow aloft (on the periphery of the ridge), where there \nwill be occasional chances of showers and thunderstorms from \nremnant convection or nearby frontal boundaries. Currently, the \nhighest chance of this occurring is around midweek (Tuesday and \nWednesday).\n\nMahale",
